        }
      ],
      "greedy_continuation": " pollen. The pollen is carried on the bees' legs and is deposited on the stigma of the flower. The pollen is then transferred to the next flower.\n\nThe bee's tongue is a modified proboscis. The proboscis is a long, thin tube that is used to suck ne",
      "samples": [
        " pollen. Pollen gets carried to the next flower, and so on until it reaches the stamen. The stamen opens to release its pollen into the air. The stamen may be lying on the flower, or may be held in the mouth.\n\nA bee behaves like an airplane on",
        " pollen. They are not alone in doing so; birds and mammals likewise use a ready-made medium of pollen to move from flower to flower. Although there are other plants which do not have pollen, they all depend on beewills to carry it to them. One of the most famous examples of",
        " pollen. Pollen contains a substance called _pollen grains_ that contain one of two different kinds of pollen. _Pollinators_ are the kind that pollinate plants. There are two kinds of pollination, viz, _self_ \\- and _interspecific_. This means that"
      ],
